# ADR-0018: Recognize source repositories by exact manifest identity

## Context

Trace input selection currently treats a root package named `musubix3` as the
musubix source repository. The current repository is named `musubix5`, so its
owned Skill declarations are omitted. Replacing the literal with `musubix5`
alone would fix this repository but would classify any consumer that reused the
package name as a source repository. Removing legacy recognition would also
change the pinned musubix3 compatibility-source behavior.

Once Skill Markdown is selected, annotation-shaped examples can be mistaken for
real declarations. The Skill documents contain executable trace declarations
and instructional code examples, so excluding the whole Markdown file or
ignoring all Markdown comments would lose authoritative nodes.

## Decision

Recognize a musubix source repository only by one of two exact root-manifest
pairs:

- package name `musubix5` and object-form repository URL
  `https://github.com/nahisaho/musubix5.git`; or
- package name `musubix3` and object-form repository URL
  `https://github.com/nahisaho/musubix3.git`.

Do not normalize repository shorthands, protocols, suffixes, or casing for this
selector. Missing or malformed manifests and all partial matches are consumer
states and do not throw. This predicate controls only Skill Markdown inclusion;
normative artifacts and supported programming-language sources retain their
existing selectors.

Before the generic annotation matcher processes a path satisfying
`isSkillSource`, apply a deterministic line-preserving Markdown mask. This mask
replaces, rather than precedes, the generic quote/string mask for Skill
Markdown, because prose apostrophes or quotation marks must not hide later
authoritative declarations. Never apply Markdown rules to programming-language
or YAML inputs.

Mask in this order:

1. Fenced blocks whose opener has zero to three leading spaces and at least
   three backticks or tildes. A closer has zero to three leading spaces, uses
   the same character, and has a run at least as long as the opener. Ignore the
   info string. Mask both delimiter lines. An unterminated fence masks through
   EOF.
2. Top-level indented blocks that begin at document start or after a line that
   was blank in the original text, with at least four spaces or one tab. When a
   preceding nonblank line exists, require it to begin at column zero and not
   be a list marker matching `[-+*]` or `\d+[.)]` followed by whitespace. Mask
   consecutive indented lines and internal originally blank lines, stop before
   the first originally nonblank line without the required indentation, and
   exclude trailing blank lines from the block. A masked fence line never
   becomes a synthetic blank-line trigger, and indentation that continues a
   paragraph or list item is not a new code block.
3. Inline spans in remaining unmasked text. Delimiters are equal-length
   backtick runs, the first equal-length run before the next line that was blank
   in the original text closes the span, and an opener without such a closer
   masks nothing.

Replace every masked character except `\r` and `\n` with a space, preserving
the total JavaScript string length, CRLF shape, a leading BOM, source offsets,
and reported line numbers. Ignore a leading BOM only while recognizing the
first line's fence or indentation structure. Discover candidate comment ranges
and test for `@id`, `@implements`, `@verifies`, or `@design` using the searchable
masked slice. Parse every Skill node ID and annotation target from that masked
slice as well; use original text only to calculate the preserved line location.
This prevents an opener or real annotation outside a code example from
capturing a masked closer, ID, or target inside it. Keep the existing annotation
grammar and diagnostics outside masked regions. Implement the limited
code-example scanner locally instead of adding a Markdown parser dependency or
attempting full CommonMark rendering.

## Rejected alternatives

- Match only `package.json.name === "musubix5"`: rejected because a consumer
  can reuse that package name and would then trace bundled implementation
  Skills.
- Match the Git working-tree origin: rejected because trace input collection is
  also used in copied fixtures and source archives, and origin lookup would add
  process and environment dependence.
- Add a consumer-controlled `trace.skillSources` configuration flag: rejected
  because it would let consumers silently promote bundled implementation Skills
  into strict trace inputs and would change evidence scope through configuration
  rather than an approved source-repository identity.
- Remove musubix3 recognition: rejected because the pinned compatibility source
  remains an approved analysis target.
- Parse every Markdown construct with a new CommonMark dependency: rejected
  because only code-example exclusion is required and a dependency would
  enlarge the runtime and packaging surface.
- Ignore all annotations in Markdown: rejected because repository Skill
  declarations are authoritative trace nodes.

## Consequences

The current repository and pinned legacy source include their owned Skill
declarations, while ordinary consumers fail closed to the prior exclusion
behavior. Repository trace nodes, edges, fingerprints, and dependent evidence
change when Skill files change.

The local Markdown scanner must preserve length and newlines and is deliberately
limited to fenced, indented, and inline code examples. Tests must cover both
recognized identities; missing, unreadable, malformed, non-object, and
non-string manifest fields; declaration discovery outside code examples;
prose apostrophes and quotation marks before real declarations;
stray prose comment openers before fenced annotation examples;
mixed comments containing a real annotation plus a fenced example whose IDs and
targets must remain excluded;
three-space-indented and unterminated fences; longer valid closers;
original-text blank-line classification; top-level indented blocks and list
continuations plus exact block termination; single- and double-backtick spans;
unmatched backticks; unchanged indented annotations in non-Markdown inputs;
first-line BOM recognition, CRLF, and stable line locations; absence of
`CODE-FEATURE-001` and
`TEST-FEATURE-001`; and strict resolution of the real repository's Skill
declarations.

